    "# Tutorial 4: Logistic Regression\n",
    "## Move from continuous predictions to probabilities and outcome groups\n",
    "\n",
    "**Course:** IE 1171  \n",
    "**Level 1 file:** `SocialNetworkAdClicks.csv`  \n",
    "**Level 2 file:** `iris.csv`  \n",
    "**Level 1:** Required core—binary logistic regression  \n",
    "**Level 2:** Optional deep dive—multinomial logistic regression\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "Tutorials 2 and 3 modeled a continuous response. Tutorial 4 introduces **classification**, where the response places each observation into one of a small number of outcome groups, also called classes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The required analysis predicts whether a social-network user purchased after seeing an advertisement. Claude will draft the Python, but you remain responsible for defining the groups, deciding which variables belong in the model, checking the probability threshold and performance measures, and examining the consequences of group-based predictions.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "98dd3965",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# <img src=\"tutorial-icons/learning_objectives.png\" alt=\"Learning Objectives\" width=\"44\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:10px;\"> Learning Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "By the end of this tutorial, you should be able to:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Understand classification as predicting groups**\n",
    "   - Distinguish a continuous prediction from assignment to an outcome class.\n",
    "   - Identify the response groups, predictors, predicted probabilities, and predicted classes.\n",
    "   - Explain how the sigmoid function keeps a binary probability between 0 and 1.\n",
    "2. **Build a logistic-regression workflow**\n",
    "   - Explain why a tracking identifier is not a meaningful predictor.\n",
    "   - Create repeatable model-fitting and evaluation partitions.\n",
    "   - Standardize continuous predictors and fit the model with Claude-generated Python.\n",
    "3. **Evaluate group predictions and their consequences**\n",
    "   - Interpret a confusion matrix, complementary performance measures, and ROC AUC.\n",
    "   - Explain how a threshold redistributes false positives and false negatives.\n",
    "   - Extend the same probability framework from two outcome groups to three Iris groups.\n"
   ]
