    "# <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"44\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:10px;\"> Theory Foundation: From Linear Scores to Probabilities and Decisions\n",
    "\n",
    "For a binary response $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$, logistic regression models the conditional probability $p(x)=P(Y=1\\mid X=x)$. It first forms a linear score $\\eta=\\beta_0+x^T\\beta$, then applies the sigmoid function\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(x)=\\sigma(\\eta)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\eta}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Equivalently, the log-odds are linear:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\log\\frac{p(x)}{1-p(x)}=\\beta_0+x^T\\beta.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore $e^{\\beta_j}$ is an odds ratio for a one-unit increase in $X_j$, holding included predictors fixed. Odds are $p/(1-p)$, not probabilities; doubling the odds does not usually double $p$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Coefficients are estimated by maximizing the Bernoulli likelihood or, equivalently, minimizing log loss:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "-\\ell(\\beta)=-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left[y_i\\log p_i+(1-y_i)\\log(1-p_i)\\right].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Confident wrong probabilities receive a large penalty. Complete separation can drive coefficient estimates toward infinity, and correlated predictors can make individual coefficients unstable. Regularization can control this instability.\n",
    "\n",
    "A probability becomes a class only after choosing a threshold $t$: predict 1 when $\\hat p\\ge t$. Sensitivity $=TP/(TP+FN)$ and specificity $=TN/(TN+FP)$ move in opposite directions as $t$ changes. Precision $=TP/(TP+FP)$ also depends on prevalence, so it can shift across populations even if sensitivity and specificity stay similar. ROC AUC ranks positive cases above negative cases across thresholds; calibration asks whether cases assigned probability 0.7 are positive about 70% of the time. Neither metric chooses the acceptable consequences of false positives and false negatives.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Questions you should be ready to answer\n",
    "\n",
    "- Why does logistic regression model log-odds rather than probability directly?\n",
    "- What is the difference between a coefficient, an odds ratio, and a risk difference?\n",
    "- Why is 0.50 not automatically the correct threshold?\n",
    "- How are discrimination and calibration different?"
   ]
  },
