    "# Part 1: Define the Binary Classification Groups\n",
    "\n",
    "The file `SocialNetworkAdClicks.csv` contains:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Column | Role in this tutorial | Meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `User ID` | Tracking identifier | Label used to distinguish user records |\n",
    "| `GenderBinary` | Supplied demographic group predictor | Male = 1 and female = 0 in the course file |\n",
    "| `Age` | Continuous predictor | User age |\n",
    "| `EstimatedSalary` | Continuous predictor | Estimated salary |\n",
    "| `Purchased` | Outcome group | Purchased after the advertisement: yes = 1, no = 0 |\n",
    "\n",
    "The response has two outcome groups:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Y = \\texttt{Purchased} \\in \\{0,1\\}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The predictors are `GenderBinary`, `Age`, and `EstimatedSalary`. `User ID` is excluded because an identifier is meant to tell records apart; it does not describe a stable characteristic that should generalize to new users. A model can otherwise learn accidental patterns in record numbers.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three uses of the word **group** must stay separate:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. `Purchased = 0` and `Purchased = 1` are the **outcome groups being predicted**;\n",
    "2. `GenderBinary` is a **supplied demographic group code used as a predictor**;\n",
    "3. the training and test sets are **row partitions used to fit and evaluate the model**.\n",
    "\n",
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Why Logistic Regression Fits Two Outcome Groups\n",
    "\n",
    "Ordinary linear regression can produce values below 0 or above 1. Logistic regression instead models the probability that a row belongs to the positive outcome group:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(X)=P(Y=1\\mid X).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "It transforms a linear score through the sigmoid function:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p(X)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "z=\\beta_0+\\beta_1X_1+\\cdots+\\beta_pX_p.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The probability is converted into a predicted group only after a threshold, such as 0.50, is chosen.\n"
   ]
