    "# Part 3: From Probabilities to Predicted Classes\n",
    "\n",
    "Logistic regression naturally produces a probability for each class.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a binary model:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "returns the estimated probability of class `1`, which here means `Purchased = 1`.\n",
    "\n",
    "The default class prediction usually applies a threshold of 0.50:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\widehat{Y}=\n",
    "\\begin{cases}\n",
    "1, & \\widehat{p}\\geq 0.50\\\\\n",
    "0, & \\widehat{p}<0.50\n",
    "\\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "A probability is not the same thing as a class. Keeping both visible makes the decision rule auditable.\n"
   ]
  },
