    "# Part 4: Evaluate Classification Performance\n",
    "\n",
    "A confusion matrix separates four outcomes:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Actual | Predicted | Name |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 0 | 0 | True negative |\n",
    "| 0 | 1 | False positive |\n",
    "| 1 | 0 | False negative |\n",
    "| 1 | 1 | True positive |\n",
    "\n",
    "From these counts:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Sensitivity}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Specificity}=\\frac{TN}{TN+FP}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Precision}=\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Accuracy summarizes all correct predictions, but it does not show which class is being missed. ROC AUC evaluates how well the model ranks positive observations above negative observations across thresholds.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: One Confusion Matrix, Several Group Questions\n",
    "\n",
    "ISLP Section 4.4.2 shows why classification performance cannot be reduced to one number. Sensitivity asks what share of the actual positive group was found. Specificity asks what share of the actual negative group was rejected correctly. Precision starts from the predicted positive group and asks how often that prediction was correct.\n",
    "\n",
    "The ROC curve plots sensitivity against `1 - specificity` over many thresholds. ROC AUC summarizes ranking across those thresholds; it does not prove that the predicted probabilities are calibrated or that the model treats demographic groups fairly.\n"
   ]
  },
