    "# Part 5: Thresholds Are Decisions\n",
    "\n",
    "A threshold is not a law of nature.\n",
    "\n",
    "Lowering the threshold usually:\n",
    "\n",
    "- predicts more positives;\n",
    "- increases sensitivity;\n",
    "- decreases specificity;\n",
    "- creates more false positives.\n",
    "\n",
    "Raising the threshold usually does the opposite.\n",
    "\n",
    "The appropriate tradeoff depends on the use. An advertising system may tolerate a wasted impression differently from a system deciding access to housing, credit, employment, or health services.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: A Threshold Turns Probability Into Treatment\n",
    "\n",
    "The fitted model supplies a probability, but the threshold determines which outcome group is assigned. Lowering the threshold moves more rows into the predicted-positive group; raising it moves more rows into the predicted-negative group. Choosing a threshold therefore requires an explicit objective and an account of who bears each type of error.\n"
   ]
  },
