  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "49a2a3bb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# AI for Social Good: Classification Changes Access, Attention, and Voice\n",
    "\n",
    "The earlier IE 1171 course connected classification to human-centered AI and algorithmic accountability. Real systems can influence hiring, healthcare, public services, advertising, and content moderation. In those settings, a classifier does more than label an observation—it can change what a person is offered, denied, shown, or asked to prove.\n",
    "\n",
    "A responsible classifier should:\n",
    "\n",
    "- report probabilities and uncertainty rather than present labels as facts;\n",
    "- examine **false positives and false negatives separately** because their costs are rarely equal;\n",
    "- compare error and calibration across relevant groups;\n",
    "- question whether protected traits or proxies are driving outcomes;\n",
    "- include affected stakeholders when choosing thresholds and acceptable trade-offs;\n",
    "- provide understandable notice, human review, and a path to challenge consequential decisions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Human-centered design matters because technical teams may not know which error is most harmful to the people affected. A threshold that looks optimal on a metric can still silence a marginalized group or exclude people from an opportunity.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Social-good principle:** Classification creates a rule for treating cases differently. The people affected by that rule belong in the evaluation of it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "942ef3bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Tutorial 4 Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "You used Claude to inspect a two-group outcome, distinguish tracking and demographic variables, create reproducible row partitions, standardize the continuous predictors, fit a multiple logistic-regression model, generate probabilities, convert them into predicted groups, and compare threshold-dependent errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main lesson is that logistic regression produces group probabilities. A threshold and an application context turn those probabilities into decisions about people.\n"
   ]
